            "review_title": "Review at Korin",
            "review_text": "Trang N\n1 contribution\n0\n### KORIN 20 DONG DU: HOW A NEW JAPANESE RESTAURANT IS REDEFINING “AUTHENTIC” IN THE HEART OF DISTRICT 1\nSep 2026 • Family\nI have always found Japanese cuisine most compelling when it knows when to step back and let the ingredients speak for themselves. That was probably the first impression I had of Korin, Japanese Contemporary Gastronomy House, at 20 Dong Du in the heart of District 1.\n\nWhat caught my attention was not simply the premium ingredients, but the way they are treated. Bluefin tuna, Hokkaido scallops, Murasaki uni and Japanese black abalone are carefully selected according to their origin and season, allowing their natural character to remain at the centre of each dish.\n\nThe same restraint comes through in the kitchen. Korin’s 48-hour Matsusaka Beef consommé, for instance, develops a remarkably clear yet deep flavour, while the Matsusaka Beef Ultimate Crispy Grill brings together a crisp exterior and tender centre without overwhelming the meat itself.\n\nI also enjoyed the rhythm of the menu. Sashimi leads into grilled abalone, handmade soba and Matsusaka Beef, before moving towards Hokkaido hairy crab rice and a contemporary dessert. Each course feels like part of a considered progression rather than a collection of individually impressive dishes.\n\nAnd then there is light.\n\nReflections on gold leaf, the sheen of a glaze, and the soft glow from the presentation boxes subtly change the way each dish is perceived. It is a visual layer that makes sense of Korin’s philosophy: **“Where Light Reveals Flavor.”**\n\nFor me, what makes Korin interesting is this balance between contemporary presentation and a very Japanese respect for ingredients. Rather than reinventing authenticity, it seems to ask a simpler question: what happens when you give exceptional ingredients enough space to speak?\n\nAt 20 Dong Du, that makes Korin a new address worth discovering for anyone who appreciates Japanese cuisine beyond the familiar.\n\nIf you could choose just one ingredient to begin your Korin experience, what would it be?\nRead more\nWritten September 18, 2026",
